@page "/calendar-agenda"

<CatHead Title="Agenda and editing">
    The list view of a calendar, the hours a site is shut and the stretches that colour a day, the day
    behind "+2 more", and events the reader drags to another day or time and stretches to end later. The
    library paints and reports; your app keeps every date and moves every event.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-drag-move-2-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">A drop is an event, never a move</span>
        Dragging an event writes attributes on your markup and dispatches <code>sedna-drop</code> with the
        slot it landed on; resizing writes one custom property while the grip moves and dispatches
        <code>sedna-resize</code>. Your handler changes the event's date in your own data and renders it
        there. Nothing the library does survives a render it did not ask for.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Calendar.Agenda" Title="An agenda" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.cal-agenda</code> is a list of days, each a <code>.cal-agenda-day</code> with a
        <code>.cal-agenda-head</code> &mdash; the weekday, then the date at the end &mdash; and a
        <code>.cal-agenda-events</code> of <code>.cal-agenda-event</code> rows: a
        <code>.cal-agenda-time</code>, a <code>.cal-event-dot</code>, a <code>.cal-agenda-title</code> and a
        line of <code>.cal-agenda-meta</code>. A row takes the event hues, <code>.cal-event--go</code> to
        <code>--teal</code>, for its dot; <code>.cal-agenda-event--past</code> mutes one that is over. Today
        is <code>aria-current="date"</code> on its day. A day with nothing on is a
        <code>.cal-agenda-empty</code> in your own words &mdash; or leave the day out, which is yours to
        choose. The headings stay pinned while their day scrolls under them.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Calendar.AgendaPanel" Title="An agenda in a narrow panel" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Under 30rem of its own width &mdash; a side panel, a phone &mdash; the time moves above the title
        and the column goes, so the title keeps the width. The agenda measures itself, not the window, so
        it does the same in a narrow card on a wide screen. Put it in a <code>.sedna-scroll</code> with a
        height and each day's heading stays at the top while that day is in view. In a card,
        <code>.cal-agenda--flush</code> gives up the agenda's own border and corners, so the card's are
        the only frame &mdash; the same modifier every framed calendar view takes.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Calendar.BusinessHours" Title="Business hours and background events" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.cal-closed</code> hatches the hours outside business hours, at <code>--cal-start</code> for
        <code>--cal-span</code> hours like a block, and <code>.cal-day-col--closed</code> a whole day.
        <code>.cal-event--background</code> on a <code>.cal-block</code> is a stretch that is true of the
        time rather than an appointment &mdash; a stock freeze, a maintenance window &mdash; drawn full
        width in its hue, under the blocks. Both take no pointer, so what is under them stays clickable.
        <strong>Say the hours and the meaning in words too</strong>: a hatch and a tint are not read out.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Calendar.BackgroundDays" Title="Background events across days" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        On a month, <code>.cal-event--background</code> is a pill with no lane: give it the days with
        <code>grid-column</code> and no <code>grid-row</code>, write it before the events, and it tints the
        whole of those days under them, its title in the day's head beside the number. In a
        <a href="/calendar#a-resource-timeline">resource timeline</a> it covers the row the same way, its
        title in the bottom row.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Calendar.MoreEvents" Title="The rest of a busy day" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open "+2 more".</strong> Make the <code>.cal-more</code> a
        <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with <code>data-popover</code>, and write the popover: a
        <code>.popover-title</code> naming the day, then every event of that day as a
        <code>.cal-event-list</code> of the same pills, each on a line of its own. List them all, not only
        the hidden ones &mdash; the reader asked to see the day. The button's label says how many more and
        which day, because "+2 more" alone does not.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.CalendarMonthMove" Title="Move an event to another day" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a pill, or focus one and press <kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd>.</strong> Each
        <code>.cal-day</code> is a drop slot: <code>data-drag-zone</code> naming its date,
        <code>data-drag-axis="slot"</code> and the calendar's <code>data-drag-group</code>. Each week's
        <code>.cal-row-events</code> is the zone the pills come from, with <code>data-drag-accept=""</code>
        so nothing lands back in it, and each pill is a <code>data-drag-item</code>. A pill dropped on top
        of another day's pill still lands on that day.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>sedna-drop</code> names the day it landed on as <code>To</code> and the day it was picked up
        from as <code>FromSlot</code>; move the event by the distance between them, and a four-day event
        held by its third day keeps that day under the pointer. From the keyboard the arrows move it a day
        across or a week down, by where the days are on screen, and the sentences the reader hears are
        yours, in <code>data-drag-live</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.CalendarWeekEdit" Title="Move and resize on a time axis" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a block to another time or day, or drag its bottom edge.</strong> A
        <code>.cal-slots</code> in each <code>.cal-day-col</code>, written first, holds one
        <code>.cal-slot</code> per half hour &mdash; <code>--cal-slot-hours</code> for another step &mdash;
        each a drop slot naming its time. Slots in the closed hours are <code>aria-disabled="true"</code>,
        which refuses a drop there. The column is the zone the blocks come from.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.cal-resize</code> is the grip on a block's end: a <a href="/table-columns">resize handle</a>
        on <code>--cal-height</code>, stepped by a slot's height, with <code>data-resize-key</code> naming
        the event. <code>sedna-resize</code> gives the length in pixels; turn it into an end time and
        render the block's new <code>--cal-span</code>. Focus the grip and the arrow keys do the same,
        and keep its <code>aria-valuetext</code> saying the end time.
    </p>
</CatExample>
